17. Actualization

Until you make the unconscious conscious, it will direct your life and you will call it fate. 
(Carl Jung, The Integration of the Personality)

Who/what should we trust, if not God or Reason? Yourself? If only it was that simple. Much of what you are is foreign.
	The worst part of yourself is who you resemble most. The key is to bring your worst close to good, even if it means engaging in a little revelry, accepting your dark side, bringing it up as it brings you down; befriending your Shadow, laughing at its jokes, seeing the hypocrisy of complete piety; but also showing it the perils of evil, the absurdity of wickedness, until you appreciate each other as complex, neither black nor white, and are able to discuss a meeting with Virtue—if only it would see a part of you in it. 
	For quicker results, find the person most Opposite of you, who you somewhat despise, but are intrigued by nonetheless. If they are not too evil, and have a tincture of good, this will be your best friend. They will despise you too, but for opposite reasons. This is the Jungian spark, clash, that allows truths to be revealed that neither want to hear. Then find the person in your life your Shadow hates; this will be the person doing psychological damage, the person you admire, but are not intrigued by. The reason, the Shadow wants you as you are, the Other wants you to change into something better. This Angel will also hate your Shadow. This is the test. If you want to shed, actualize, follow your Shadow. If you want to transform into something else, follow your Angel. If you don’t know yourself, follow both, until one likes you more. If two shadows they will destroy each other, if two angels they will become the same. Finally, for everyone in between, you will neither despise nor admire them; this is good in that they will have no impact on your life. The person you both admire and are intrigued by should be yourself.
	Of course I am being somewhat facetious in what I said above. But in pursuing self-actualization you do need to look at the people in your life. Other people see you in a way that you cannot. And this is probably where we should begin with Jung's process of individuation—or integrating the unconscious with the conscious.  In taking on the psyche the first Jungian aspect to look at is the Persona. This is the social mask we wear; an artificial personality that we share with the world. Now there are many masks we wear rather the one; some more artificial than others. We will need to shed these masks in the process of individuation. (We can of course rebuild our masks later.) This is important to do because masks can remove the need for growth. Many people are comfortable with adopting ready-made masks as a substitute for their personality. While this is a type of personality, it is a very shallow one. How to shed our masks? Ideally, you can appeal to friends for help. Friends can usually dismantle many of your masks, even if you are unaware of their severity or their existence at all. The mask(s) you share with your friends is much harder to dispose of. This requires brutal honesty and candor, and should be reserved for those closest to you.  A person may also have some exotic mask(s) used in private reflection or fantasy. These masks will also need to be shed—at least while pursuing the process of individuation. 
	The next Jungian aspect is the Ego-consciousness.  Instead of a mask, this is more like a mirror. It is how you see yourself or would like to see yourself. Usually it includes things that you take to be positive or acceptable. But in many cases what people see in the mirror is distorted and not an accurate representation of who they really are. People will often inflate their personal image of themselves. This can lead to narcissism and megalomania. Similarly, creating some unrealistic ideal that does not match what's actually possible can lead to frustration and dissatisfaction. There is also the other end, where people see themselves negatively and worse than they really are. This is also destructive. In following the path of individuation, we must accurately capture our personal image in a way that coheres to objective reality. In doing so, we must disband the ideal self (or some conception of a future ideal self) and grasp the conscious self in the present, as it currently is, unadulterated. (That is not to say we cannot create some new better conscious self later on.) This requires some humility and self-love. Sometimes what you find may not be satisfying, even boring. But this should not be a major hurdle. The ego-consciousness is only a part of what you are. The complete Self is much bigger and likely more interesting. 
	The third Jungian aspect is the Personal Unconscious. This contains all of a person's repressed or forgotten memories as well as psychological complexes.  The goal in individuation is to recover as much of this hidden content as possible. This is pertinent because unconscious memories and complexes can influence behavior in unexpected ways. In order to have more psychological agency we will thus need to bring the personal unconscious to the surface. That being said, recovering the personal unconscious can involve reexperiencing significant trauma or traumatic events. It is therefore recommended to pursue this step of individuation with the help of a psychotherapist. This can mitigate and control trauma, as well as reduce confusion and emotional turmoil. It also allows for the utilization of advanced therapeutic techniques like reframing, word association, hypnosis, and guided imagery.
	After recovering as much of the personal unconscious as possible (it's nearly impossible to recover it all) we can then move on to the fourth Jungian aspect: the Collective Unconscious. Within the Collective Unconscious are two important components: the Anima/Animus and the Shadow. The first, the Anima/Animus, if you recall, represents the feminine aspects of a man and the masculine aspects of a woman. In the process of individuation we will need to draw out the opposite sex. In discovering our Anima or Animus we can make use of different archetypes to help us along. For example, we may understand or appreciate femininity by looking to general archetypes like the Mother or Maiden. We might also use more specific feminine archetypes like Pandora, or the Virgin Mary, or Joan of Arc, or Athena—to better relate to women. From the other side, a woman may appeal to masculine archetypes like the Father, or Hero, or Hercules, or Jesus, or Casanova, or Socrates. In comparing and opening oneself up to opposites we become more whole.
 	Though, I admit, it could be argued that in the modern context sharp divisions based on sex are less fixed than they used to be. I take this as a good thing, and something that makes the process of individuation easier. But, I suggest, we might take the process further than Jung proposed and use this step of individuation as representing our relation to and reconciliation with Otherness. We can view ourselves as other in order to better appreciate our individuality. And even in the modern context there are hurdles to overcome. For instance, we may deal with the Opposite, not as a different sex, but rather as a different racial group or ethnicity or social class. It thus might prove useful to see ourselves in a different light, and may help alleviate bias in the same way that discovering the Anima/Animus can alleviate sexism.       
	The next component is the Shadow. This, again, represents our dark side. It is the desires, urges, feelings, thoughts, that we are ashamed of and hide from others. We usually suppress this part of ourselves and try not to acknowledge it; in many cases hiding it from ourselves as well. This proves destructive and can result in us unconsciously projecting our negative qualities onto others. For Jung, the Shadow is not bad or entirely negative.  It can be a source of creative energy. It can also be a source of power and confidence. The process of individuation will require us to come to terms with the Shadow. It is a big part of who we are, and finding the Shadow might be seen as the most important part of individuation.  
	This step of the process of individuation usually starts with an encounter with the Shadow. This can't necessarily be forced but is likely to come naturally at some point if we follow the previous steps of individuation put forward. The Shadow will take the form of a distinct archetype and will reoccur. In the initial encounter, usually in a dream, the Shadow archetype will challenge you as falling short of getting the things you truly desire. This will likely cause uneasiness. From here, the conversation should continue and can be enhanced by various methods like dream analysis, active imagination, creative expression, and mandalas. Using images from mythology proves particularly useful. 
	As the Shadow becomes known, a person will discover and become aware of his/her repressed desires, urges, feelings, thoughts, etc. To continue the process of individuation a person might pursue different courses of actions. The first course is to dissolve one's guilt and/or shame and actively pursue and acquire what one truly wants. Considering that many social conventions, traditions, taboos are unfounded, this may not be as devastating as a person fears. This could foster courage and individual growth in the face of others' opinions. Of course, there are some desires, actions that are clearly wrong and should not be pursued. This leads to a negotiation with the Shadow over how much liberty should be allowed in pursuing what one wants. Ideally, over time, a sort of compromise or harmony can be reached where room is allowed for the satisfying of (non-heinous) desires within a reasonable scope, resulting in a type of integration where the unconscious is brought to the surface and acknowledged by the conscious. As for those desires, urges, feelings, thoughts that are too bane to be granted a conscious sanction, they need not be re-suppressed and sent back into obscurity.  These dark features of us can be worked out and understood by engaging with various archetypes, leading to an ongoing process of integration: "shadow work" as is the parlance of analytical psychology. 
	Finally, I would say there is an even deeper level of the unconscious which might be called the Forbidden Unconscious. This goes beyond individuation, and I propose it only as a philosophical exercise to better understand the mind. Going too deep can be dangerous and a psychological hazard. To get to this point, we must first recognize the Shadow. Only then should we proceed. With the Shadow's blessing we should then go further down to the workings of the mind that are not really a part of you at all and are inhuman. In achieving this level, we might rely on prolonged active imagination, deep meditation, automatic or free writing, psychedelic drugs, fervor, and temporarily inducing psychosis. The individuation process leaves us bare and open, allowing us to pursue archetypes to the point where they become independent entities, which we might call psychic phantoms. These phantoms will usually take on the form of some religious or mythical figure or a dead relative, but can manifest in many different ways. What's unique is that these phantoms will claim to be more than useful archetypes but rather agents with something to offer you; usually some sort of arcane knowledge. If you are lured by this promise, the phantom will remain outside of your conscious control and persist in the psyche. Whether to negotiate with a phantom depends on what it has to offer. Sometimes it will offer something valuable, sometimes it won't. There are indeed gifts to be had hidden deep within the mind. But the more time spent in this netherworld the more one runs the risk of having a psychotic break or developing permanent psychosis.   
	The above journey is useful for understanding the mind. But the Unconscious, to be clear, can consume a person if left unchecked. The Shadow is a reflection of you, but further down there are real Monsters, all claiming to be guides. If you don't know yourself, trust yourself, and look back; that beautiful  creature will take your place. As the Red Book shows, Jung never made it back the same. I think if there is any truth to what we would call the paranormal or paranormal activity this is the Gateway. Here we will find ghouls, demons, angels, ghosts, spirits, lesser gods, fairies, and jinn. This could pose some interest. But some things are best left alone. Be warned, after surveying Tartarus and seeing it for yourself once or twice, hastily leave and never return again. 
	With this behind us we have the psychic material necessary to at last create the Self. But while the personal process of individuation or actualization is complete it is worth mentioning that external factors can cripple or sabotage this development. One of the most powerful influences is the culture we find ourselves immersed in. It does vary, and certain societies will increase our potential whereas others will diminish it. But I recommend severing our link to arbitrary social conventions. This is not saying one should be a social outcast. But rather that there should be some distance between the internal creation and public opinion. It is thus prudent to recreate the Persona and the various masks needed to navigate one's society. In this way we can protect the complexities of the unique individual from unjust pressure. 
	Somewhat more difficult is to sever the link between one's values and society's sanctioned moral codes. Nearly every society is tainted by fluctuating class interests and power dynamics. It is thus not the most reliable source for determining what is right. I would propose that our moral code be derived from philosophical reflection. But it need not be something to include within the process of Self-building. A more pressing worry is that in most societies morality is weaved in with or derived from religion. This is the biggest threat to our individuality. In fact, it is quite perplexing why religion, God, would care whether you are moral or not, or enforce it. The ruse; I take it, is that it is to keep you away from your Shadow, which tends to be a little evil; prone to violating social norms. Finding your Shadow is one of the most important steps to self-actualization. Of course, God doesn’t want this, and will snare you into a moral code—guilt to back. The proselytizing of morality by religion is not (as we believe) for the benefit of mankind, it is for the benefit of God; to keep you ensnared. What we call piety is really just a psychic trap. There’s only one moral rule that you need to follow, which I will give later on.  
	I have beaten this dead horse. But escaping God, in whatever form He takes, is one of the most important things we need to do in our lives. It is not mere inactivity that is required or resisting some faithful urge. God is embedded in the very structure of our minds. He is the onus aeternus. The opposite of actualization is submission or surrender. This unfortunately is the end of Self. They simply are not compatible. This relinquiship is also present historically in the structure of philosophy, and is something that must be overcome. Both individually, and as philosophers, we must fight for authenticity and what truly is our own. 


18. Fall from Grace

Science is the great antidote to the poison of enthusiasm and superstition. 
(Adam Smith, The Wealth of Nations—1776)

A Brief History of Philosophy. Philosophy has an illustrious history. Tracing the Western tradition, from Socrates onward, Philosophy enjoyed special prominence and influence for well over a thousand years. And our present liberal culture is tied to this success. Philosophical Schools like Plato’s Academy, Aristotle’s Lyceum, and Epicurus’ Garden acted as epicenters of learning in Athens, driving philosophical and scientific debate throughout the Hellenic world. Later Neo-Platonism and Skepticism spread throughout Europe and North Africa forcing rigorous examination and adjustments to prevailing theories and religious dogma. Stoicism made its way into the Roman Empire, appealing to both slave and emperor alike. Epicureanism found its place with commoners. Mass adoption of philosophical theories, new and old, pushed their way throughout the Empire and spread to its corners. Early Christians like Saint Augustine incorporated elements from prominent philosophical theories into the foundations of the budding new religion: Refashioned Christianity. The great philosophical debates travelled east to the Roman Byzantine Empire. 
	Though Philosophy's unique status would come to a tragic halt with the fall of the Roman Empire and the beginning of the Dark Ages. Philosophy managed to linger on, but only by eventually being absorbed into Religion. This proved to be fortuitous, though less than ideal and perversive for the tradition. But, I confess, we owe those studious monks and scribes, in Christian Europe as well as the Islamic East, a huge debt. The tradition would have surely been lost if not put under their care. 
	Philosophy survived the Dark Ages and toward its end even had a significant boost in the hands of Thomas Aquinas.  Aristotle was reworked and fashioned as a theological weapon. “The Philosopher” became an authority on everything. This elevated status would continue and gain a second boost into the Renaissance as everything Ancient Greek became the craze. Philosophy reached a high point, not held since the Early Roman Empire. 
	But Philosophy’s revered position would change as we entered the Enlightenment. With the advent of Science, everything religious, supernatural, arcane became intellectual taboo. The Spirit of Philosophy, caught in the middle, split from Religion and decided to side with the Newcomer. But it did so at a cost. Instead of Champion, It became lesser maid to Science. It would eventually be cast aside, and lose its status, as Reason became equated with the scientific. The new standard of propagation (of ideas) became usefulness. And Science promised to explain everything. 
	Lost. Not being useful, Philosophy searched for its niche. The inner world called to it; rationalism, dualism, idealism; but by the time it gained hold it was already too late. These things were already reclaimed by Religion; and even those defending it as philosophers were doing so on religious grounds. Not being able to return back to Religion, being cut off, Philosophy did the only thing it could, and declared itself a science in its own right. This was the beginning of the end. 
	There is an alternate history that I ought to mention; One of Theologians sparked with philosophical sight who came close to discovering the true Nature of God—as Manipulator. Only to have if stripped away, concluded, under the watch of George Berkeley —a sin that would discourage all further inquiry. I call this near miss the Theological Flirt. The lost discovery: that we are, for the most part, mentally determined—by God among other things—but not physically determined. 
	In sum: Early Christian philosophers, riding Neo-Platonism, removed God from the Mountain and put him out there. But as God became more abstract so did his power, eventually dissipating into a single (mental-physical) causal principle. 
	Medieval philosophers renewed God’s particular powers and experimented with God being in our heads, but were always quick to also throw him back into the world, and reassert his physical power. They were never quite comfortable with mere mind-control (the truth); and in the end removed God from the head entirely calling it his covenant of “Free Will.” 
	It took quite a number of years before philosophers thought to ask the question: ‘Why couldn’t God just make us do stuff directly? Why bother with physical causation?' The next fling would occur with the Theological-Idealists, who would throw us in the Mind of God. We finally had something resembling true mental determinism. But the cost was our physical freedom. We were fully determined again. This was the last scandal before the 1,500-year affair ended entirely, the truth lost. And this, unfortunately, would be the last time the Spirit of Philosophy tried to save Religion from itself; before it moved on forever, and left It to its own devices—damned.


19. Rising Star

God is dead.
(Friedrich Nietzsche, The Gay Science—1882) 

Philosophy is dead.
(Stephen Hawking, The Grand Design—2010)

In the Modern Age, Science has grown to eclipse Religion and Philosophy in status. Its success has been unrivaled. Even those who are religious largely accept scientism, and revere the discipline. It holds the potential, we think, to explain everything. Did Science kill Philosophy? I say No. Philosophy has been soul searching for 200 years. It has yet to find its place. But I think it is time for it to do what it was always supposed to do, what it has been afraid to do for 2,500 years. It is time for Philosophy to replace Religion and become Master of the Internal World.
	Now, throughout its long history, Philosophy has learned a lot and has been considerably developed. I think philosophy currently has enough content to draw from, old and new, to be functionally complete for its purposes—acknowledging that it is far from perfect and that we still need to sort through and make sense of this vast content. (If we include the non-Western philosophical traditions there is even more content to utilize.) Largely, there is enough material to work with, and philosophy is equipped for the task at hand. 
	There is really no need to wait for some better future Ideal Philosophy, or for some new yet-to-be-born Great Philosopher, to radically change the philosophical paradigm. The history of philosophy has no shortage of geniuses, and much of what matters philosophically has already been argued for or suggested or hinted at by one of them at some point. Lesser-known philosophers of history are also being made accessible, in case something slipped through the cracks. There is also the work and collaboration of all our current professional philosophers, rapidly throwing ideas back-and-forth to each other with a click. The time is ripe for Philosophy to make, or start making, its Big Move. That is, taking its rightful place as Master of the Internal World. 
	Though I do acknowledge that philosophy is typically much broader than the study of the internal. But, I strongly believe, it is important to claim a definitive niche and defend it, even if it means narrowing the field—and giving ground to science. Not doing so in the past, as was pointed out in the previous section, proved a disaster for Philosophy. Not to mention that a niche-less philosophy, as of late, proves an easy target for scientists, like Hawking, to attack and discredit. Let us not risk the Spirit of Philosophy dying on account of something as simple as not defining its proper domain. 
	By saying Philosophy should become Master of the Internal World I am not simply suggesting that some new (philosophical) religion be formed to usurp existing religions. I am instead saying Philosophy can offer something qualitatively better than religion. That is, by the end of the essay, the reader should have a direct means to achieving spiritual fulfilment, rather than being manipulated (either by others or by yourself or by God) into having spiritual fulfilment or getting it indirectly or accidentally or partially—if at all.  
	Philosophy is different in kind from religion. Faith itself is not something philosophy can authentically endorse. There must be reasons, preferably good or compelling reasons, for believing philosophical positions. And when this doesn't hold, we should put our beliefs to the fire (rather than put disbelievers to the fire; a very effective historical strategy for resolving religious disputes). That is, skepticism rather than dogmatism is the starting position. In this way, philosophy is more like science than religion. But there are important demarcations. 
	Philosophy, to be clear, is not science or a science; nor should it try to be. Its method is deductive, not inductive. Its goal is internal, not external. It also deals with things science cannot, or even should not deal with. The stuff of good philosophy borders on the absurd, and it takes a very special breed of person—philosophers—to move it forward; or backwards. Scientists have more realistic matters to attend to. But I cheer, it's about time for Philosophy to take on the spooky, outlandish, weird, bizarre, esoteric, delirious. The Enlightenment scare is over. The most obvious and easiest starting points are to accept the possibility that rationalism, dualism, idealism are true—as suggested throughout the essay. To take up this old set of philosophical challenges. But to do it free of Religion and Science—and their many commitments and snags. Philosophy should be pure and hold its own. 
	By holding its own, I am not saying Philosophy is opposed to Science. On the contrary, they need each other. We need philosophy to give us a full theory of the internal world, and we need science to give us a full theory of the external world. Because the World is internal and external we need both to truly understand Everything. But while Philosophy and Science are teammates playing for the human species, they should be respected as masters of separate domains. They should not be married. There has been a marriage for 200 years, and it has been an unhappy one, at least on the part of philosophy. They should cordially divorce but remain good friends; occasionally friends with benefits, unintended pregnancies and all, with the new fields it produces. 
	Where should we place Religion in all this? I say let it die. It has never done anything but make us miserable and servile. Philosophy and Science are able to make sense of the world. The only reason to hold onto religion would be to fill some spiritual hole—that science is unable to fill. But I believe philosophy can fill that hole. We do not need religion to control our lives. All we need is a small dose of mysticism, just enough confuse to make life interesting, marvelous, beautiful, sublime. My philosophical mysticism, I believe, provides that esoteric whiff, meaning, inner vision, purpose necessary to satiate us—as spiritual beings. And it does so while avoiding the drawbacks and pitfalls present in most religions. 
	All that being said, Philosophy and Science are not the only players in the world of ideas. The Artist is also needed. There is a standard division of labor where the artist proposes new ideas, the philosopher defines it, and the scientist explains it. But artists tend to be afraid to overreach, rather only occasionally borrowing concepts. Though some science fiction writers have done work that could fall under the category of science. (In fact, I could be accused of dabbling in some fiction myself in the coming sections.) There is no reason why art and philosophy ought not work together, even in capacities much more meaningful than the interplay which occurs between science and science fiction. For some reason, historically, this never really happened in any substantial sense. The Artist has always watched Philosophy at a distance, inspired, but never mustering the courage to court it or treat it on equal footing—besides, again, humbly borrowing concepts. Now that Philosophy is free, It sees you. Mainly, the Artist should take on philosophy in a serious way. More beauty is needed in philosophy. 
	The Artist once assisted Religion. Most religions would not be possible without the artists who crafted its poetry and wonderful stories—and statues, and architecture, and paintings, and songs. Any religion without its art would likely not be accepted, much less survive for very long. I take it, it is time for the Artist to now help Philosophy craft its own poetry and wonderful stories. This aesthetic aid is crucial if Philosophy has any real chance of replacing Religion. A new romance is needed. And who knows—unlike with Science—Philosophy and Art may turn out to be true soul mates.
